# Lucent environment template (M0.6)
#
# Copy to `packages/server/.env` and fill in the values you need. That is the
# file the server reads on boot (`packages/server/src/main.ts` loads `../.env`
# relative to itself). A copy at the repo root is read by nothing: `docker
# compose` uses a root `.env` only for `${VAR}` substitution inside
# docker-compose.yml, and Vite only exposes `VITE_*` keys to the client.
#
# Nothing here is read at build time; all keys are picked up on boot (and a few
# by the dev launcher / the web dev server's proxy).
#
# Conventions:
#   - LUCENT_* keys are Lucent's own configuration.
#   - ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY are read directly because that's the
#     convention the upstream SDKs use.
#   - The "default" column shows what the server does when the key is absent.
#   - "Required in prod" means the server refuses to boot without it.
#   - The "Dev tooling" and "Testing" sections are read by plain Node
#     processes (scripts/dev.mjs, packages/web/vite.config.ts, vitest), which
#     see only the real environment. Export those in your shell; putting them
#     in packages/server/.env has no effect.

# ─── Server bind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_HOST (default 127.0.0.1) — interface the HTTP+WS server binds to.
#   Use 0.0.0.0 inside Docker so the host browser can reach the container.
# LUCENT_PORT (default 4387)     — loopback port the server listens on.
# LUCENT_WEB_ORIGIN (default http://localhost:5173) — used for CORS allowlist
#   and to set the CSRF cookie's domain. Must match the URL the browser hits.

# ─── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_REQUIRE_AUTH (default ON, in every environment) — when on, every
#   request except signup/login/health needs a session cookie or bearer token.
#   Set it to exactly "0" to opt out for solo localhost work; any other value,
#   or an absent key, means on. It deliberately does not key off NODE_ENV
#   (see the comment in packages/server/src/config.ts): a container started
#   without NODE_ENV=production used to serve every board to anyone who could
#   reach the port.
# LUCENT_SESSION_SECRET (required in prod, >=16 chars) — HMAC key for session
#   tokens. Rotate = invalidate every session. Generate with
#   `node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`.
# LUCENT_AUTH_LIMIT_PER_WINDOW (default 10) — max login/signup attempts per
#   IP+email tuple in the limiter window.
# LUCENT_AUTH_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS (default 300000 = 5 min) — limiter window.

# ─── Persistence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_DATABASE_URL — postgres://user:pass@host:port/db. When set, the
#   server runs migrations on boot and uses Postgres-backed user/board
#   stores. When unset, in-memory stores are used (single-process, no
#   restart survival).
# LUCENT_ASSET_BACKEND (memory | disk | s3) — where asset bytes live.
#   Memory is tests-only, disk is single-node dev (needs LUCENT_ASSET_DIR).
#   Production is s3, which additionally needs LUCENT_S3_BUCKET,
#   LUCENT_S3_REGION (or AWS_REGION), LUCENT_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and
#   LUCENT_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (or the AWS_* equivalents), plus optional
#   LUCENT_S3_ENDPOINT for S3-compatibles. Metadata always lives in
#   Postgres when LUCENT_DATABASE_URL is set.

# ─── Realtime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_REALTIME_TICKET_TTL_MS (default 60000) — how long a WebSocket auth
#   ticket is valid before the handshake must complete.

# ─── AI provider ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_AI_PROVIDER (openai | anthropic | mock) — picks the LLM provider.
#   "openai" or "gorouter" also covers OpenAI-compatible gateways.
# LUCENT_AI_BASE_URL — OpenAI-compatible base URL (e.g. https://api.gorouter.app/v1).
# LUCENT_AI_KEY      — bearer token for the gateway.
# LUCENT_AI_MODEL    — model id. Default differs per provider; see provider.ts.
# LUCENT_AI_NAME     — display name for the gateway ("gorouter", "openrouter", …).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY  — required when LUCENT_AI_PROVIDER=anthropic.
# OPENAI_API_KEY     — fallback used by the image provider if LUCENT_IMAGE_KEY
#   is not set.

# LUCENT_AI_TIMEOUT_MS (default 90000) — abort an LLM call after this many ms.

# ─── Server-hosted diarizing STT (docs/25 V1, fourth STT slot) ───────────────
# LUCENT_STT_PROVIDER (default http) — "http" speaks the generic sidecar
#   contract below; "deepgram" uses Deepgram pre-recorded transcription
#   (`POST https://api.deepgram.com/v1/listen`, `Token` auth) with
#   buffer-at-close sessions (REST+diarization correctness: per-chunk labels
#   would restart per request and merge strangers); "openai" uses
#   `POST {base}/v1/audio/transcriptions` (multipart, Bearer key) the same
#   buffered way. Any other value fails loud at boot — misconfiguration
#   must not masquerade as privacy. Deepgram is live-verified; OpenAI is a
#   reviewed stub awaiting a keyed live run.
# LUCENT_STT_KEY (+ LUCENT_STT_URL for http) — the credential. Key without a
#   working backend means the route answers 503, never a silent void. For
#   "http": a diarizing speech sidecar the server proxies chunked audio to
#   (`POST {URL}/transcribe`, Bearer key, `{ finals: [...] }` JSON back).
#   For "deepgram": the Deepgram project key (`LUCENT_STT_URL` overrides the
#   default `https://api.deepgram.com` when set).
# LUCENT_STT_NAME (optional, http only) — display name in metering/logs.
# LUCENT_DEEPGRAM_MODEL (default nova-3), LUCENT_DEEPGRAM_CONTENT_TYPE
#   (default audio/webm — set audio/wav when feeding WAV), LUCENT_STT_TIMEOUT_MS.
# LUCENT_STT_STREAMING=1 (Deepgram only) selects the live-streaming
#   session: finals per chunk + VAD onset instead of buffer-at-close.
#   Default off until streaming soaks; same key, same mapping tiers.
# LUCENT_OPENAI_STT_MODEL (default gpt-4o-transcribe-diarize) +
#   LUCENT_OPENAI_CONTENT_TYPE for provider=openai; the key falls back to
#   OPENAI_API_KEY when LUCENT_STT_KEY is unset.
#   Keys stay server-side, the same rule as every other provider key. An org
#   can forbid this route with the `serverStt: "disabled"` org setting even
#   when it is configured here.
#
# ─── Server voice synthesis (docs/25 V7, M6.2) ─────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_TTS_PROVIDER (default http) — "http" speaks the generic sidecar
#   contract (`POST {URL}/v1/audio/speech`, `{input, voice?}` → audio bytes);
#   "deepgram" uses Aura TTS (`POST /v1/speak?model=…`); "openai" uses
#   `POST {base}/v1/audio/speech` (`{model, input, voice}`). Unconfigured means
#   the route answers 503 and the client falls back to local speech.
# LUCENT_TTS_KEY (falls back to LUCENT_STT_KEY — one Deepgram project key
#   serves every API; OpenAI falls back to OPENAI_API_KEY) + LUCENT_TTS_URL
#   for http. LUCENT_DEEPGRAM_TTS_MODEL (default aura-2-thalia-en),
#   LUCENT_DEEPGRAM_TTS_ENCODING/SAMPLE_RATE (optional; linear16/16000 for
#   PCM work), LUCENT_OPENAI_TTS_MODEL (default tts-1) + LUCENT_TTS_VOICE
#   (default alloy), LUCENT_TTS_TIMEOUT_MS. An org
#   can forbid this route with the `serverTts: "disabled"` org setting.

# LUCENT_ALLOW_BYO_KEYS (default off) — set to exactly "1" to let callers
#   present their own provider API key per request (`x-lucent-provider-key`
#   header; see packages/server/src/modelRouter.ts). The key is used for one
#   upstream call and dropped, never stored. This is the deployment CEILING an
#   org's provider policy can only tighten, never widen: an org saying "any"
#   does not unlock BYO here, and an org saying "byo-forbidden" locks the key
#   out even when this is on (docs/24 Phase C).
# LUCENT_AI_SELF_HOSTED (default off) — set to exactly "1" to DECLARE that the
#   configured provider above runs on infrastructure the customer controls.
#   The server cannot probe a provider's endpoint, so this is an operator
#   attestation, not a detection; an org's `self-hosted-only` provider policy
#   reads it, and a BYO key is never self-hosted regardless (the BYO path
#   targets fixed public cloud endpoints).

# ─── Image provider (optional, for diagram illustrations) ────────────────────
# LUCENT_IMAGE_KEY      — bearer token for image generation.
# LUCENT_IMAGE_BASE_URL — base URL for the image API.
# LUCENT_IMAGE_MODEL    — model id.
# LUCENT_IMAGE_NAME     — display name.
# LUCENT_IMAGE_TIMEOUT_MS — abort an image request after this many ms. A
#   positive integer; anything else (0, negative, text) counts as unset. Unset
#   falls back to LUCENT_AI_TIMEOUT_MS, then the provider default (120000).
#   It is PER MODEL ATTEMPT: when LUCENT_IMAGE_MODEL lists fallbacks (or
#   LUCENT_IMAGE_FALLBACK_MODEL is set), each model gets a fresh timeout, so a
#   request can take up to (number of models) × this before it fails.

# ─── Vector provider (optional, docs/26 §6.3: Icons & vectors → Generate) ───
# LUCENT_RECRAFT_API_KEY — Recraft API token. Set it and generation uses Recraft
#   (about 80 API units = $0.08 per variation on the default model); without it
#   the server uses an offline mock. Server-side only; never ship it to a client.
# LUCENT_RECRAFT_MODEL   — default recraftv4_1_vector (any Recraft *_vector model).
# LUCENT_VECTOR_PROVIDER — "mock" to force the mock, "off" to disable generation.

# ─── Rate limits ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_RATE_PER_MINUTE (default 30) — generate-image / ai-text per client.
# LUCENT_RATE_WINDOW_MS  (default 60000) — sliding window size.
# LUCENT_QUOTA_PER_DAY   (default 1000) — daily cap per client.

# ─── Mail (M1.3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_MAIL_TRANSPORT (default console) — "console" prints to stdout, "smtp"
#   dials LUCENT_SMTP_URL, "http" posts to a provider's REST API. A
#   half-configured transport (transport set, credential missing) falls back to
#   console rather than refusing to boot.
# LUCENT_SMTP_URL — smtp://user:pass@host:port (or just host:port).
# LUCENT_SMTP_USER / LUCENT_SMTP_PASSWORD — SMTP AUTH PLAIN credentials.
# LUCENT_MAIL_FROM (default noreply@lucent.local) — envelope From.
# LUCENT_MAIL_API_KEY — API key / server token for the "http" transport. The
#   transport is only selected when this is set.
# LUCENT_MAIL_ENDPOINT (default https://api.resend.com/emails) — absolute URL
#   the "http" transport posts to; switching providers is config, not code.
# LUCENT_MAIL_AUTH_HEADER (default Authorization) — header the key travels in
#   (Postmark wants X-Postmark-Server-Token).
# LUCENT_MAIL_AUTH_SCHEME (default Bearer) — prefix before the key. May be set
#   empty, which is what a bare token header needs.
# LUCENT_MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS (default 10000) — "http" transport request timeout.

# ─── Dev tooling (shell env only — the server never reads these) ──────────────
# LUCENT_SERVER_PORT (default 4387) — port `npm run dev` starts the API server
#   on; scripts/dev.mjs passes it to the server as LUCENT_PORT.
# LUCENT_WEB_PORT (default 5173) — port the Vite dev server binds to. strictPort
#   is on, so a clash fails loudly instead of silently moving.
# LUCENT_API_PORT (default 4387) — where Vite's /api and /ws proxies point.
#   scripts/dev.mjs sets it from LUCENT_SERVER_PORT so one variable moves both.
# LUCENT_SERVER_URL (default http://127.0.0.1:<LUCENT_API_PORT>) — overrides the
#   whole /api proxy target, e.g. when the API runs on another host.
# LUCENT_WS_URL (default ws://127.0.0.1:<LUCENT_API_PORT>) — same, for /ws.

# ─── Testing (shell env only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_TEST_DATABASE_URL — postgres://… used by the Postgres-backed suites
#   (packages/server/test/pg-*.test.ts and friends). When unset those suites
#   skip instead of failing, which is why `npm test` is green with no database.
#   Point it at a throwaway database: the suites create and drop tables.

# ─── Misc ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LUCENT_MAX_WORKSPACE_BYTES (default 8388608 = 8 MiB) — refused with HTTP 413
#   if a workspace JSON blob exceeds this on POST /api/boards.
# LUCENT_TRUST_PROXY (default off) — when on, X-Forwarded-For is trusted for
#   the auth limiter's client key. Only enable behind a trusted reverse proxy.
# LUCENT_METRICS_VERBOSE (default off) — include message bodies in /metrics.
# LUCENT_LOG_LEVEL (DEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR) — NOT WIRED UP YET.
#   `logLevelFromEnv()` in packages/server/src/obs/logger.ts parses it, but
#   nothing imports that module yet, so setting it changes no behaviour today.
#   Listed because the key exists in the code, not because it works.
# NODE_ENV (default development) — "production" flips secure defaults on.

# ─── MCP agent access (packages/mcp) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# All three set → the MCP server talks to a REAL Lucent board over HTTP
# (RemoteBoardSession): `get_board` reads the server's board, and
# `apply_canvas_operations` lands through POST /api/boards/:id/ops, the
# same validated pipeline the WebSocket room uses. Any unset → the MCP
# server runs its process-local sandbox (BoardSession), which needs no
# server at all — the try-it-with-zero-setup mode.
# LUCENT_MCP_URL — server base URL, e.g. http://127.0.0.1:4387 (no trailing
#   slash needed; one is tolerated).
# LUCENT_MCP_TOKEN — a bearer session token. Get one from the signup/login
#   response body's `token` field (POST /api/auth/signup returns it); it is
#   the same 7-day credential a programmatic client uses.
# LUCENT_MCP_BOARD — the board id the session operates on (brd_…).
# The token's user must have EDITOR-or-above access to that board; viewer
# tokens are refused with the same 404 masking every board route applies.
#
# Smoke, once the server is running on 4387:
#   LUCENT_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4387 \
#   LUCENT_MCP_TOKEN=<token from signup> LUCENT_MCP_BOARD=<brd_id> \
#   npx tsx --conditions=development packages/mcp/src/main.ts
# then paste a tools/call line on stdin:
#   {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"get_board","arguments":{}}}

# ─── LiveKit hosted voice (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# All three set → POST /api/livekit/token mints per-room, per-user JWTs
# (the route the web's LiveKit STT provider already calls; its Settings
# placeholder is this exact path). Unset → the route 503s and the web uses
# on-device STT (Web Speech / Whisper), which is the zero-vendor default.
# LUCENT_LIVEKIT_API_KEY — the public key from the LiveKit console.
# LUCENT_LIVEKIT_API_SECRET — the secret half. SERVER-ONLY: minting happens
#   in livekit.ts and the secret never reaches the browser.
# LUCENT_LIVEKIT_URL — wss://… project URL clients connect to (works for
#   LiveKit Cloud or a self-hosted server; token format is identical).
# Tokens are 10-minute HS256 JWTs scoped to the board's room; the client
# re-fetches on every session start, so there is no refresh flow to build.
# LUCENT_VOICE_AGENT_NAME (default lucent-voice-agent) — the worker name the
#   Live call dispatches to; the voice-agent worker registers under the same
#   variable. Workers sharing a name share the jobs, so a developer's worker
#   on a LiveKit project that production also uses MUST set its own name
#   (e.g. lucent-voice-agent-dev-<you>), or it will answer real users' calls.
#   The worker also needs DEEPGRAM_API_KEY (or LUCENT_STT_KEY) and the AI
#   provider variables above: `npm run dev -w @lucent/voice-agent`.

# --- Integrations: OAuth connect and Slack (docs/26 A5) ---------------------
# Linear, Jira and GitHub connect with a key the server holds: the owner
# names an environment variable in Admin → Integrations, never the key.
# Linear and Jira can instead connect with OAuth ("Connect with Linear"),
# which needs BOTH of these:
# LUCENT_TOKEN_KEY — 32 random bytes, base64 (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`).
#   Seals the tokens the providers hand over (AES-256-GCM, bound to their
#   org); without it the server offers no OAuth, and a malformed one stops
#   the boot. Losing it means every OAuth connection has to connect again.
# LUCENT_LINEAR_CLIENT_ID / LUCENT_LINEAR_CLIENT_SECRET — a Linear OAuth app;
#   its callback is <LUCENT_WEB_ORIGIN>/api/integrations/oauth/linear/callback
# LUCENT_JIRA_CLIENT_ID / LUCENT_JIRA_CLIENT_SECRET — an Atlassian 3LO app
#   (scopes read:jira-work write:jira-work read:jira-user offline_access);
#   callback <LUCENT_WEB_ORIGIN>/api/integrations/oauth/jira/callback
# Slack posts each meeting's summary to a channel: create an incoming
# webhook, put its URL in a variable (any name), and connect Slack in Admin
# with that name. Only https://hooks.slack.com/services/… is accepted.
